---
title: Multiple Interrupts
id: interrupts-multiple
order: 3
description: "Render a queue of pending decisions and resolve them item by item or all at once as one validated batch."
keywords:
  - tanstack ai
  - ag-ui interrupts
  - resolveInterrupts
  - batch approval
  - cancelInterrupts
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One run can pause on several decisions at once. A tool approval and a generic\nmiddleware request can land in the same batch. You want to show the whole queue\nand send the answers back together, not one round trip each."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"two-ways-to-resolve","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two ways to resolve"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have already seen the first one on the "},{"type":"link","href":"./tool-approval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Approval"}]},{"type":"text","value":" page:\ncall a method on the item itself."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Per item: resolve each one where you render it.\ninterrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When several client-owned items are pending, it is often easier to answer them\nall from one place. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" hook gives you root helpers for those items:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// All at once: one callback decides every pending item.\nresolveInterrupts((interrupt) => {\n  if (interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n    interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)\n    return\n  }\n  interrupt.cancel()\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both stage local drafts. Nothing goes to the server until every pending item has\nan answer. Then the client submits the whole set at once. The server validates\nthe whole set before it accepts any item."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When persistence is enabled, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptStore.commitBatch()"},{"type":"text","value":" can commit the\nvalidated batch in one database transaction. Without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commitBatch()"},{"type":"text","value":", the\ncompatibility fallback writes items in sequence. That fallback is not atomic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a mixed batch, switch on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind"},{"type":"text","value":". Registered generic items use their\ndefinition id to narrow their payload and response. Tool approvals keep their\nown controls. A generic item with a valid raw binding has untyped controls and\njoins the resume batch. An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"unbound"},{"type":"text","value":" item has a missing, malformed, or\nunsupported binding. It remains visible, has no controls, and does not block\nthe resumable items."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"render-the-queue","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render the queue"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Map over "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" and switch on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind"},{"type":"text","value":". Each item carries its own\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"canResolve"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errors"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/decision-queue.tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { transferTool } from '../tools/transfer'\n\nexport function DecisionQueue() {\n  const { interrupts, resolveInterrupts, cancelInterrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'account-42',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  if (interrupts.length === 0) return null\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <p>{interrupts.length} decision(s) needed</p>\n\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (\n          interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n          interrupt.toolName === 'transfer'\n        ) {\n          return (\n            <article key={interrupt.id}>\n              <p>\n                {interrupt.originalArgs.amount} to{' '}\n                {interrupt.originalArgs.recipient}\n              </p>\n              <button\n                disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n                onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n              >\n                Approve\n              </button>\n              <button\n                disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n                onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(false)}\n              >\n                Reject\n              </button>\n            </article>\n          )\n        }\n        return <article key={interrupt.id}>Unsupported: {interrupt.kind}</article>\n      })}\n\n      <button onClick={() => resolveInterrupts(true)} disabled={resuming}>\n        Approve all\n      </button>\n      <button onClick={() => cancelInterrupts()} disabled={resuming}>\n        Cancel all\n      </button>\n    </section>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resolve-every-item-from-one-callback","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolve every item from one callback"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupts(callback)"},{"type":"text","value":" runs your callback once per resumable item in a\nsingle synchronous transaction. This includes valid raw external generic\nbindings. It must resolve or cancel every such item. If it throws or leaves one\nunanswered, nothing submits:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"resolveInterrupts((interrupt) => {\n  if (interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n    interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true, { payload: { note: 'Batch review' } })\n    return\n  }\n  interrupt.cancel()\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two shortcuts cover the common cases:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupts(true)"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupts(false)"},{"type":"text","value":" approves or rejects the\nwhole queue. It works only when every item is a tool approval that needs no\npayload or edits. Generic items, mixed queues, or required payloads are\nrejected."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cancelInterrupts()"},{"type":"text","value":" cancels every resumable item with no payload, including\nclient-tool execution steps that are hidden from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onInterruptStateChange"},{"type":"text","value":" source to apply different policies to restored\n("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hydrate"},{"type":"text","value":") and current-session ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"live"},{"type":"text","value":") batches. Cancellation still applies to\nevery resumable item in the batch."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-an-answer-is-wrong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When an answer is wrong"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A bad answer does not tear down the queue. The item keeps your last valid draft,\nshows what went wrong, and lets you fix it and resubmit. Errors come in two\nplaces, and you render both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each item carries its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"errors"},{"type":"text","value":": a bad payload, invalid edited args, or an\nexpired item. The root "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interruptErrors"},{"type":"text","value":" carries failures for the whole batch:\ntransport problems, server rejections, and errors for the internal client-tool\nsteps that never show up as their own item."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This component renders both, gates its buttons correctly, and offers the two\nrecovery paths:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// app/robust-queue.tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Move money between accounts',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    recipient: z.string(),\n    amount: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ receiptId: z.string() }),\n}).client()\n\nexport function RobustQueue() {\n  const { interrupts, interruptErrors, retryInterrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'account-42',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  // Retry only helps a transport failure. Expired or stale batches cannot\n  // be retried, so do not offer it for those.\n  const canRetry = interruptErrors.some((error) => error.code === 'transport')\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (\n          interrupt.kind !== 'tool-approval' ||\n          interrupt.toolName !== 'transfer'\n        ) {\n          return null\n        }\n\n        // canResolve reflects the schema and binding, not the live phase, so\n        // also gate on the item's status and the run being busy.\n        const busy = interrupt.status === 'submitting' || resuming\n\n        return (\n          <article key={interrupt.id}>\n            <p>\n              {interrupt.originalArgs.amount} to{' '}\n              {interrupt.originalArgs.recipient}\n            </p>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || busy}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n            >\n              Approve\n            </button>\n            <button disabled={busy} onClick={() => interrupt.clearResolution()}>\n              Start over\n            </button>\n\n            {/* Item errors: bad payload, bad edited args, expired. */}\n            {interrupt.errors.map((error) => (\n              <p key={`${error.code}:${error.path?.join('.') ?? ''}`}>\n                {error.message}\n              </p>\n            ))}\n          </article>\n        )\n      })}\n\n      {/* Batch errors: transport, server, and hidden client-tool steps. */}\n      {interruptErrors.map((error) => (\n        <p key={error.code}>{error.message}</p>\n      ))}\n      {canRetry ? (\n        <button onClick={() => retryInterrupts()} disabled={resuming}>\n          Retry\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two recovery paths, side by side:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupt.clearResolution()"},{"type":"text","value":" drops one item's draft so the user can answer it\nagain from scratch. Fixing a form and calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveInterrupt"},{"type":"text","value":" again works\ntoo, the draft is replaced, not stacked."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"retryInterrupts()"},{"type":"text","value":" retries a staged batch after a transport failure. With\nsequential durable storage, a failed request may have committed earlier\nentries already. Reload the current pending records, then retry only the\nremaining batch. It does nothing for expired or stale batches, start a fresh\nrun to get a new set of interrupts for those."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"two-ways-to-resolve","text":"Two ways to resolve","level":2},{"id":"render-the-queue","text":"Render the queue","level":2},{"id":"resolve-every-item-from-one-callback","text":"Resolve every item from one callback","level":2},{"id":"when-an-answer-is-wrong","text":"When an answer is wrong","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nOne run can pause on several decisions at once. A tool approval and a generic\nmiddleware request can land in the same batch. You want to show the whole queue\nand send the answers back together, not one round trip each.\n\n## Two ways to resolve\n\nYou have already seen the first one on the [Tool Approval](./tool-approval) page:\ncall a method on the item itself.\n\n```ts ignore\n// Per item: resolve each one where you render it.\ninterrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)\n```\n\nWhen several client-owned items are pending, it is often easier to answer them\nall from one place. The `useChat` hook gives you root helpers for those items:\n\n```ts ignore\n// All at once: one callback decides every pending item.\nresolveInterrupts((interrupt) => {\n  if (interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n    interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)\n    return\n  }\n  interrupt.cancel()\n})\n```\n\nBoth stage local drafts. Nothing goes to the server until every pending item has\nan answer. Then the client submits the whole set at once. The server validates\nthe whole set before it accepts any item.\n\nWhen persistence is enabled, `InterruptStore.commitBatch()` can commit the\nvalidated batch in one database transaction. Without `commitBatch()`, the\ncompatibility fallback writes items in sequence. That fallback is not atomic.\n\nFor a mixed batch, switch on `kind`. Registered generic items use their\ndefinition id to narrow their payload and response. Tool approvals keep their\nown controls. A generic item with a valid raw binding has untyped controls and\njoins the resume batch. An `unbound` item has a missing, malformed, or\nunsupported binding. It remains visible, has no controls, and does not block\nthe resumable items.\n\n## Render the queue\n\nMap over `interrupts` and switch on `kind`. Each item carries its own\n`canResolve` and `errors`:\n\n```tsx\n// app/decision-queue.tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { transferTool } from '../tools/transfer'\n\nexport function DecisionQueue() {\n  const { interrupts, resolveInterrupts, cancelInterrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'account-42',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  if (interrupts.length === 0) return null\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <p>{interrupts.length} decision(s) needed</p>\n\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (\n          interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n          interrupt.toolName === 'transfer'\n        ) {\n          return (\n            <article key={interrupt.id}>\n              <p>\n                {interrupt.originalArgs.amount} to{' '}\n                {interrupt.originalArgs.recipient}\n              </p>\n              <button\n                disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n                onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n              >\n                Approve\n              </button>\n              <button\n                disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n                onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(false)}\n              >\n                Reject\n              </button>\n            </article>\n          )\n        }\n        return <article key={interrupt.id}>Unsupported: {interrupt.kind}</article>\n      })}\n\n      <button onClick={() => resolveInterrupts(true)} disabled={resuming}>\n        Approve all\n      </button>\n      <button onClick={() => cancelInterrupts()} disabled={resuming}>\n        Cancel all\n      </button>\n    </section>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Resolve every item from one callback\n\n`resolveInterrupts(callback)` runs your callback once per resumable item in a\nsingle synchronous transaction. This includes valid raw external generic\nbindings. It must resolve or cancel every such item. If it throws or leaves one\nunanswered, nothing submits:\n\n```ts ignore\nresolveInterrupts((interrupt) => {\n  if (interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n    interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true, { payload: { note: 'Batch review' } })\n    return\n  }\n  interrupt.cancel()\n})\n```\n\nTwo shortcuts cover the common cases:\n\n- `resolveInterrupts(true)` / `resolveInterrupts(false)` approves or rejects the\n  whole queue. It works only when every item is a tool approval that needs no\n  payload or edits. Generic items, mixed queues, or required payloads are\n  rejected.\n- `cancelInterrupts()` cancels every resumable item with no payload, including\n  client-tool execution steps that are hidden from `interrupts`.\n\nUse the `onInterruptStateChange` source to apply different policies to restored\n(`hydrate`) and current-session (`live`) batches. Cancellation still applies to\nevery resumable item in the batch.\n\n## When an answer is wrong\n\nA bad answer does not tear down the queue. The item keeps your last valid draft,\nshows what went wrong, and lets you fix it and resubmit. Errors come in two\nplaces, and you render both.\n\nEach item carries its own `errors`: a bad payload, invalid edited args, or an\nexpired item. The root `interruptErrors` carries failures for the whole batch:\ntransport problems, server rejections, and errors for the internal client-tool\nsteps that never show up as their own item.\n\nThis component renders both, gates its buttons correctly, and offers the two\nrecovery paths:\n\n```tsx\n// app/robust-queue.tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Move money between accounts',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    recipient: z.string(),\n    amount: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ receiptId: z.string() }),\n}).client()\n\nexport function RobustQueue() {\n  const { interrupts, interruptErrors, retryInterrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'account-42',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  // Retry only helps a transport failure. Expired or stale batches cannot\n  // be retried, so do not offer it for those.\n  const canRetry = interruptErrors.some((error) => error.code === 'transport')\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (\n          interrupt.kind !== 'tool-approval' ||\n          interrupt.toolName !== 'transfer'\n        ) {\n          return null\n        }\n\n        // canResolve reflects the schema and binding, not the live phase, so\n        // also gate on the item's status and the run being busy.\n        const busy = interrupt.status === 'submitting' || resuming\n\n        return (\n          <article key={interrupt.id}>\n            <p>\n              {interrupt.originalArgs.amount} to{' '}\n              {interrupt.originalArgs.recipient}\n            </p>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || busy}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n            >\n              Approve\n            </button>\n            <button disabled={busy} onClick={() => interrupt.clearResolution()}>\n              Start over\n            </button>\n\n            {/* Item errors: bad payload, bad edited args, expired. */}\n            {interrupt.errors.map((error) => (\n              <p key={`${error.code}:${error.path?.join('.') ?? ''}`}>\n                {error.message}\n              </p>\n            ))}\n          </article>\n        )\n      })}\n\n      {/* Batch errors: transport, server, and hidden client-tool steps. */}\n      {interruptErrors.map((error) => (\n        <p key={error.code}>{error.message}</p>\n      ))}\n      {canRetry ? (\n        <button onClick={() => retryInterrupts()} disabled={resuming}>\n          Retry\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe two recovery paths, side by side:\n\n- `interrupt.clearResolution()` drops one item's draft so the user can answer it\n  again from scratch. Fixing a form and calling `resolveInterrupt` again works\n  too, the draft is replaced, not stacked.\n- `retryInterrupts()` retries a staged batch after a transport failure. With\n  sequential durable storage, a failed request may have committed earlier\n  entries already. Reload the current pending records, then retry only the\n  remaining batch. It does nothing for expired or stale batches, start a fresh\n  run to get a new set of interrupts for those.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Multiple Interrupts\nid: interrupts-multiple\norder: 3\ndescription: \"Render a queue of pending decisions and resolve them item by item or all at once as one validated batch.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - ag-ui interrupts\n  - resolveInterrupts\n  - batch approval\n  - cancelInterrupts\n---\n\nOne run can pause on several decisions at once. A tool approval and a generic\nmiddleware request can land in the same batch. You want to show the whole queue\nand send the answers back together, not one round trip each.\n\n## Two ways to resolve\n\nYou have already seen the first one on the [Tool Approval](./tool-approval) page:\ncall a method on the item itself.\n\n```ts ignore\n// Per item: resolve each one where you render it.\ninterrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)\n```\n\nWhen several client-owned items are pending, it is often easier to answer them\nall from one place. The `useChat` hook gives you root helpers for those items:\n\n```ts ignore\n// All at once: one callback decides every pending item.\nresolveInterrupts((interrupt) => {\n  if (interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n    interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)\n    return\n  }\n  interrupt.cancel()\n})\n```\n\nBoth stage local drafts. Nothing goes to the server until every pending item has\nan answer. Then the client submits the whole set at once. The server validates\nthe whole set before it accepts any item.\n\nWhen persistence is enabled, `InterruptStore.commitBatch()` can commit the\nvalidated batch in one database transaction. Without `commitBatch()`, the\ncompatibility fallback writes items in sequence. That fallback is not atomic.\n\nFor a mixed batch, switch on `kind`. Registered generic items use their\ndefinition id to narrow their payload and response. Tool approvals keep their\nown controls. A generic item with a valid raw binding has untyped controls and\njoins the resume batch. An `unbound` item has a missing, malformed, or\nunsupported binding. It remains visible, has no controls, and does not block\nthe resumable items.\n\n## Render the queue\n\nMap over `interrupts` and switch on `kind`. Each item carries its own\n`canResolve` and `errors`:\n\n```tsx\n// app/decision-queue.tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { transferTool } from '../tools/transfer'\n\nexport function DecisionQueue() {\n  const { interrupts, resolveInterrupts, cancelInterrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'account-42',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  if (interrupts.length === 0) return null\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      <p>{interrupts.length} decision(s) needed</p>\n\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (\n          interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval' &&\n          interrupt.toolName === 'transfer'\n        ) {\n          return (\n            <article key={interrupt.id}>\n              <p>\n                {interrupt.originalArgs.amount} to{' '}\n                {interrupt.originalArgs.recipient}\n              </p>\n              <button\n                disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n                onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n              >\n                Approve\n              </button>\n              <button\n                disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || resuming}\n                onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(false)}\n              >\n                Reject\n              </button>\n            </article>\n          )\n        }\n        return <article key={interrupt.id}>Unsupported: {interrupt.kind}</article>\n      })}\n\n      <button onClick={() => resolveInterrupts(true)} disabled={resuming}>\n        Approve all\n      </button>\n      <button onClick={() => cancelInterrupts()} disabled={resuming}>\n        Cancel all\n      </button>\n    </section>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Resolve every item from one callback\n\n`resolveInterrupts(callback)` runs your callback once per resumable item in a\nsingle synchronous transaction. This includes valid raw external generic\nbindings. It must resolve or cancel every such item. If it throws or leaves one\nunanswered, nothing submits:\n\n```ts ignore\nresolveInterrupts((interrupt) => {\n  if (interrupt.kind === 'tool-approval') {\n    interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true, { payload: { note: 'Batch review' } })\n    return\n  }\n  interrupt.cancel()\n})\n```\n\nTwo shortcuts cover the common cases:\n\n- `resolveInterrupts(true)` / `resolveInterrupts(false)` approves or rejects the\n  whole queue. It works only when every item is a tool approval that needs no\n  payload or edits. Generic items, mixed queues, or required payloads are\n  rejected.\n- `cancelInterrupts()` cancels every resumable item with no payload, including\n  client-tool execution steps that are hidden from `interrupts`.\n\nUse the `onInterruptStateChange` source to apply different policies to restored\n(`hydrate`) and current-session (`live`) batches. Cancellation still applies to\nevery resumable item in the batch.\n\n## When an answer is wrong\n\nA bad answer does not tear down the queue. The item keeps your last valid draft,\nshows what went wrong, and lets you fix it and resubmit. Errors come in two\nplaces, and you render both.\n\nEach item carries its own `errors`: a bad payload, invalid edited args, or an\nexpired item. The root `interruptErrors` carries failures for the whole batch:\ntransport problems, server rejections, and errors for the internal client-tool\nsteps that never show up as their own item.\n\nThis component renders both, gates its buttons correctly, and offers the two\nrecovery paths:\n\n```tsx\n// app/robust-queue.tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\nimport { toolDefinition } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst transferTool = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'transfer',\n  description: 'Move money between accounts',\n  needsApproval: true,\n  inputSchema: z.object({\n    recipient: z.string(),\n    amount: z.number(),\n  }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ receiptId: z.string() }),\n}).client()\n\nexport function RobustQueue() {\n  const { interrupts, interruptErrors, retryInterrupts, resuming } = useChat({\n    threadId: 'account-42',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    tools: [transferTool] as const,\n  })\n\n  // Retry only helps a transport failure. Expired or stale batches cannot\n  // be retried, so do not offer it for those.\n  const canRetry = interruptErrors.some((error) => error.code === 'transport')\n\n  return (\n    <section>\n      {interrupts.map((interrupt) => {\n        if (\n          interrupt.kind !== 'tool-approval' ||\n          interrupt.toolName !== 'transfer'\n        ) {\n          return null\n        }\n\n        // canResolve reflects the schema and binding, not the live phase, so\n        // also gate on the item's status and the run being busy.\n        const busy = interrupt.status === 'submitting' || resuming\n\n        return (\n          <article key={interrupt.id}>\n            <p>\n              {interrupt.originalArgs.amount} to{' '}\n              {interrupt.originalArgs.recipient}\n            </p>\n            <button\n              disabled={!interrupt.canResolve || busy}\n              onClick={() => interrupt.resolveInterrupt(true)}\n            >\n              Approve\n            </button>\n            <button disabled={busy} onClick={() => interrupt.clearResolution()}>\n              Start over\n            </button>\n\n            {/* Item errors: bad payload, bad edited args, expired. */}\n            {interrupt.errors.map((error) => (\n              <p key={`${error.code}:${error.path?.join('.') ?? ''}`}>\n                {error.message}\n              </p>\n            ))}\n          </article>\n        )\n      })}\n\n      {/* Batch errors: transport, server, and hidden client-tool steps. */}\n      {interruptErrors.map((error) => (\n        <p key={error.code}>{error.message}</p>\n      ))}\n      {canRetry ? (\n        <button onClick={() => retryInterrupts()} disabled={resuming}>\n          Retry\n        </button>\n      ) : null}\n    </section>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThe two recovery paths, side by side:\n\n- `interrupt.clearResolution()` drops one item's draft so the user can answer it\n  again from scratch. Fixing a form and calling `resolveInterrupt` again works\n  too, the draft is replaced, not stacked.\n- `retryInterrupts()` retries a staged batch after a transport failure. With\n  sequential durable storage, a failed request may have committed earlier\n  entries already. Reload the current pending records, then retry only the\n  remaining batch. It does nothing for expired or stale batches, start a fresh\n  run to get a new set of interrupts for those.\n"}
